      "sequence_position": 12,
      "problem": "Let $a$ be a positive integer. Construct an expression that gives a two-digit number consisting of the last two digits of $215a.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $b=215a.$ Note that $b$ is positive. Now, let ${d}_{4},$ ${d}_{3},$ ${d}_{2},$ and ${d}_{1}$ be the last four digits of the number $a:$ \n\n$$\na=…{d}_{4}{d}_{3}{d}_{2}{d}_{1}\n$$\n\nThen, $a$ can be written in expanded form as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⋯+1000{d}_{4}+100{d}_{3}+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} \\\\  & =100(⋯+10{d}_{4}+{d}_{3})+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $a$ modulo $100:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & ≡100(⋯+10{d}_{4}+{d}_{3})+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100) \\\\  & ≡0\\cdot (⋯+10{d}_{4}+{d}_{3})+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100) \\\\  & ≡0+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100) \\\\  & ≡10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1}$ is the two-digit number consisting of the last two digits of $a.$\n\nFinally, since $b=215a,$ and \n\n$$\n215≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,100)\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100) \\\\ 215a & ≡10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100) \\\\ 15a & ≡10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression that gives the last digit of $215a$ is the following:\n\n$$\n15a\\,\\text{mod}\\,100\n$$",
